        "body": "Every forex quote shows two currencies. The first currency is the base currency and the second is the quote currency. When you read EUR/USD = 1.1800, it means 1 Euro costs 1.1800 US Dollars. So if the number rises, the Euro got stronger relative to the Dollar; if it falls, the Euro weakened.\n\nThe price you see has two sides: the bid (what buyers are willing to pay, the lower price) and the ask (what sellers are asking, the higher price). You buy at the ask and sell at the bid, and the difference between them is the spread, which is the cost your broker takes on the trade.\n\nPairs are grouped into three families. Major pairs always contain the US Dollar (EUR/USD, GBP/USD, USD/JPY, USD/CHF, AUD/USD, USD/CAD and NZD/USD) and have the lowest spreads and the most liquidity. Minor or cross pairs do not contain the US Dollar (EUR/GBP, EUR/JPY). Exotic pairs pair a major currency with a developing country currency (USD/KES, USD/ZAR, USD/TRY) and have wider spreads and lower liquidity.\n\n- The base currency is the one you buy or sell; the quote currency is what it is measured against.\n- A rising quote means the base currency strengthened; a falling quote means it weakened.\n- You buy at the ask price and sell at the bid price; the spread is your built-in cost.\n- Majors are the cheapest to trade; exotics (like USD/KES) cost more in spread and move slower.\n- Start on the pairs with the tightest spreads, such as EUR/USD, before trying anything else.",
