        "title": "Pips, lots and leverage explained",
        "minutes": 8,
        "objective": "Define a pip, learn lot sizes and their money value, and understand how leverage multiplies both profits and losses equally.",
        "body": "A pip (percentage in point) is the smallest standard unit of price movement in a currency pair. For most pairs it is the 4th decimal place, so a move from 1.1800 to 1.1805 is 5 pips. For pairs involving the Japanese Yen it is the 2nd decimal place. There is also a \"pipette\" at the 5th decimal, worth one tenth of a pip, which most platforms show.\n\nPosition size is measured in lots. A standard lot is 100,000 units of the base currency, a mini lot is 10,000 and a micro lot is 1,000. On EUR/USD a standard lot moves roughly 10 dollars per pip, a mini lot 1 dollar per pip and a micro lot 0.10 dollars per pip. This is why position size controls your risk: thousands of dollars are at stake per pip if you trade the full lot.\n\nLeverage lets you control a position much larger than your deposit. With 1:100 leverage and a 1,000 dollar account you can open a 100,000 dollar position. It is a loan from the broker secured by your margin, and it multiplies profits and losses by exactly the same factor. Traders who lose accounts lose them through leverage combined with over-sizing, never through the market alone.\n\n- A pip is the 4th decimal for most pairs and the 2nd decimal for JPY pairs.\n- 1 standard lot = 100,000 units; 1 mini lot = 10,000; 1 micro lot = 1,000.\n- Pip value scales with your lot size: always calculate it before placing a trade.\n- Leverage is borrowed buying power; it enlarges wins and losses identically.\n- Treat leverage as a sizing tool, never as a licence to trade huge positions."
