        "body": "Every trade has costs that are hidden inside the price. The spread is the difference between the bid (sell) and ask (buy) price; it is the amount price must travel before your trade breaks even. A pair quoted 1.1800/1.1803 has a 3-pip spread, and on a standard lot that is roughly 30 dollars of cost on entry.\n\nBrokers take money in two common ways. A spread-based broker embeds the cost in the price and charges no separate commission. A raw-spread or commission-based broker shows a much tighter spread (often 0.0 to 0.5 pips) and charges a fixed commission per lot, usually a few dollars each way. The only number that matters is the total cost: spread plus commission combined.\n\nThere are also holding costs. A swap (or overnight interest) is charged or credited every day you keep a position open past the nightly cutoff, based on the interest-rate difference between the two currencies. A positive swap pays you; a negative swap costs you. Avoiding these charges is one reason day traders close positions before the day ends.\n\n- The spread is your entry cost; calculate it in money, not just pips.\n- Compare brokers on total cost, not on whether they say commission or spread.\n- Swaps are daily interest charged or paid while a position is open.\n- A tight spread is worthless if the broker is unregulated or skims on execution.\n- Check fees before you deposit: deposits and withdrawals also have costs."
