        "body": "The broker is the gatekeeper to your money, so choosing one is a decision about security, not just price. Regulation is the first non-negotiable. A regulated broker is supervised by a recognised authority such as the FCA in the UK, ASIC in Australia, CySEC in Europe for EU clients, or the CMA and CBK frameworks in Kenya. Regulators require the broker to keep client funds separate (segregated accounts), publish financial reports and handle complaints.\n\nLook next at how the broker is structured. Segregated client funds mean your money cannot be used to pay the broker's own debts if it collapses, which has happened to unregulated firms. Also check the deposit and withdrawal reality: does the broker actually accept M-Pesa or bank transfer, what are the minimums, how long do withdrawals really take, and are there surprise fees? Test this by depositing the minimum and withdrawing it before funding a larger amount.\n\nFinally, compare the trading conditions that affect profitability: execution speed (do they requote or slip your orders?), the spread on the pairs you trade, platform quality (MT4/MT5), and the quality of local support. Avoid any broker that promises guaranteed profits, pays referral bonuses for members, or pressures you to deposit fast. Those are marks of a scheme, not a market access provider.\n\n- Regulation (FCA, ASIC, CySEC, CMA) is non-negotiable; check the licence number on the regulator website.\n- Client money must be held separately from company money.\n- Always test a small withdrawal before trusting the broker with big money.\n- Compare spreads on your actual pairs, plus execution and slippage.\n- Run from anything that promises \"guaranteed profits\" or fast-deposit bonus schemes."
