        "title": "Drawing your first trading plan",
        "minutes": 10,
        "objective": "Turn analysis into a written, repeatable plan: setup, entry, stop, target, size and rules for when not to trade.",
        "body": "A trading plan is the bridge between understanding and surviving. It is a written document that answers, before price moves, exactly what you will do. Start with the market and timeframe you trade and the pairs you allow. Then write your setup in the form of a checklist, not a sentence: for example, (1) daily trend up, (2) price pulled back to a tested support zone, (3) a bullish engulfing candle closed above the zone, (4) higher timeframe agrees. If any box is unticked, the trade does not exist.\n\nNext write the execution details that exist before you ever open the trade: the entry trigger, the stop loss location (always beyond the structure that invalidates the idea, never a random number), the target based on resistance or a risk-reward minimum like 1:2, and - critically - the position size fixed by the 1% risk rule, never by how confident you feel. Then add the after-the-fact rules: take a screenshot, log the trade in your journal immediately, move your stop to break even only once as a policy decision, and respect the system result over any single trade.\n\nFinally, write the do-not list. Which days and times are off-limits? What happens after two consecutive losses - a full pause, or half size? What ends your trading day at the laptop? Beginner traders fail far more often from disobeying \"when not to trade\" than from having no ideas. The plan is not a suggestion; it is the instruction manual for a machine, and you are the machine operator. Review and refine it weekly, but never rewrite it mid-trade.\n\n- A plan is a written checklist, not a good intention.\n- Define setup, entry trigger, stop, target and size before the trade.\n- Size from the 1% rule; never from confidence.\n- Write rules for when you do NOT trade; they matter most.\n- Follow the plan and critique it later; never change it mid-trade."
