        "title": "Fibonacci retracements",
        "minutes": 10,
        "objective": "Learn the key Fibonacci levels, how to draw them, and how to use them as a map of pullback zones combined with candles.",
        "body": "The Fibonacci retracement is a grid of horizontal levels drawn between a significant swing low and swing high, at the ratios 23.6%, 38.2%, 50%, 61.8% and 78.6%. These percentages come from the Fibonacci sequence, where each number is the sum of the previous two, and their ratios describe proportions found throughout nature - and, empirically, recurring patterns in markets. The tool's job is to answer one question during a pullback: where might price find renewed buyers (or sellers)?\n\nDrawing it correctly matters more than any theory. Connect the two extremes of the most recent significant swing - from the swing low to the swing high in an uptrend, or the swing high to the swing low in a downtrend - on the timeframe you trade. The levels then mark possible pullback destinations. The 38.2% is the shallow level in strong trends, 50% is the \"magic middle\" and 61.8% (the golden ratio) is the deep level that marks the line between a healthy pullback and a full reversal: a pullback holding above 61.8% keeps the trend intact, while closing below it threatens the structure.\n\nThe professional use is entirely as context, never as a standalone trigger. In an uptrend you wait for price to pull back into a Fib zone and then for a bullish candle to close there - price action confirms the zone. Combine it with support and resistance: a 50% or 61.8% retracement that lands exactly on a previous resistance-turned-support zone is stronger than either factor alone. Levels act as maps of where to expect turns, and flip into targets if broken: bulls who miss the pullback can target the next Fib level ahead.\n\n- Levels: 23.6, 38.2, 50, 61.8 and 78.6% of a significant swing.\n- Draw from swing low to swing high (up) or the reverse (down).\n- 61.8% is the golden ratio; losing it usually means the trend is broken.\n- Fibs map likely pullback zones; they never signal by themselves.\n- Combine Fib confluence with a candle close and a structural level."
