        "title": "Tax and record keeping in Kenya",
        "minutes": 7,
        "objective": "Keep accurate trading records, track currency, and seek professional advice on how trading gains are treated.",
        "body": "A profitable trading business is also a record-keeping business, and in Kenya the records work starts the day you open the first account, not at tax time. Keep a master ledger of every deposit, withdrawal, trade, spread cost, swap, commission and net result, in the currency the account trades in, from the first trade onward. The ledger is your source of truth: reconciling against your broker statement regularly catches errors early and gives you a defensible profit and loss picture.\n\nKenyan law treats trading income seriously; forex gains can constitute taxable income, and capital gains and other regimes can have their own rules, which change over time. This lesson deliberately teaches no tax figures because the correct treatment depends on your personal facts - residence status, how regularly you trade, your broker's jurisdiction and whether trading is your main income. That is exactly why the reliable instruction is: keep perfect records and consult a qualified Kenyan tax professional before you rely on any specific treatment. Getting this wrong is expensive; a professional opinion is cheap.\n\nBeyond compliance, good records are a trader's operational asset. The ledger feeds your trading journal's statistics, lets you separate genuine strategy edge from currency movement, and provides the monthly review numbers that tell you whether to scale. Use clearly named M-Pesa and bank accounts for trading money, keep all broker receipts and statements, and reconcile at least monthly. The trader who cannot produce clean records cannot prove performance - to tax authorities, or to themselves when making scaling decisions.\n\n- Open and maintain a master ledger from the very first trade.\n- Track deposits, withdrawals, costs and P&L in the account's currency.\n- Forex gains may be taxable income; the treatment depends on your facts.\n- Consult a qualified Kenyan tax professional for your precise position.\n- Reconcile with broker statements monthly; clean records are a business asset."
