        "title": "Continuing your education",
        "minutes": 6,
        "objective": "Plan lifelong learning: revisit modules, follow the economic calendar, review monthly and treat losses as tuition.",
        "body": "The market forgives one kind of mistake - the kind you learn from - and punishes everyone else. Education, then, is not the course you finished; it is a permanent operating system you refresh continuously. The practical plan has four standing commitments. First, revisit: return to these lessons after every milestone - first live trade, first losing month, first win streak - because each new context makes old material read differently. Second, follow the news: keep the economic calendar on your radar for the interest decisions, inflation prints and employment figures that move your pairs, and study how price reacted before and after the announcement.\n\nThird, review monthly. Your journal is the curriculum of the next month: answer what worked, what broke, which setups qualified and failed, whether the numbers matched your planned risk-reward. The monthly review converts one month of market data into one month of improved rules, which compounds into real advantage across a year. Fourth, protect the habit of humility: treat every loss as tuition paid in advance, trade the small positions that let you gather data cheaply, and stay suspicious of gurus selling certainty. The market has no certificate and no graduation.\n\nFinally, build an honest environment around your trading: a mentor or a small, candid community improves accountability, a library of a few quality books and courses is enough (depth beats breadth), and your demo account remains a free laboratory for testing any new idea before it meets capital. The traders who last ten years are not the smartest; they are the ones whose education never ended, whose rules stayed alive in weekly and monthly review, and whose losses were always, always tuition.\n\n- Revisit the material at every new stage; each context re-teaches it.\n- Follow the economic calendar and study price's reaction to news.\n- Run a monthly journal review; let it write next month's rules.\n- Treat every loss as tuition and test new ideas on demo first.\n- Depth over breadth: a few good sources, reviewed forever."
